{"post": {"ID": 490, "post_title": "Keyword Difficulty in SEO: What It Is and How to Use It", "post_excerpt": "What keyword difficulty is, how tools score it, what a good score is for your site and how to check a keyword step by step, with worked examples.", "post_author": "3", "post_date": "2025-02-05 16:07:38", "post_modified": "2026-11-13 16:53:12", "post_content_b64": ""}, "meta": {"_thumbnail_id": "539", "_yoast_wpseo_title": "Keyword Difficulty in SEO: Meaning and Use | Reinforce Lab", "_yoast_wpseo_metadesc": "What keyword difficulty is, how tools score it, what a good score is and how to check a keyword step by step, with worked examples. A free plain guide.", "rl_updated": "2026-11-13", "_rl_carried": "a:3:{s:7:\"prod_id\";i:216101;s:4:\"from\";s:59:\"https://reinforcelab.com/what-is-keyword-difficulty-in-seo/\";s:8:\"decision\";s:5:\"D-178\";}", "rl_type": "guide", "_yoast_wpseo_primary_category": "12", "rl_answer": "Keyword difficulty (KD) is a score, usually 0 to 100, that estimates how hard it is to reach page one of Google for a search term. Tools such as Semrush and Ahrefs calculate it from the strength of the pages that already rank, so scores differ by tool. Use it to compare keywords, then read the real results page to decide.", "rl_takeaways": "Keyword difficulty is an estimate made by SEO tools. Google does not publish it.\nScores differ between tools, so compare keywords inside one tool only.\nNew sites should start with keywords in the 0 to 30 range and move up as links and trust grow.\nAlways check search intent and the real top 10 results before you choose a keyword.\nCounting the links the lower-ranking pages have gives a more useful target than the score alone.\nRecheck your target keywords every three to six months.", "rl_faqs": "What is keyword difficulty in SEO? | Keyword difficulty is a score, usually from 0 to 100, that estimates how hard it is to rank on the first page of Google for a keyword. Higher means more competition. It is calculated by SEO tools, not by Google.\nWhat is a good keyword difficulty score? | It depends on your site. New sites do best with scores of 0 to 30. Sites with more links and trust can aim for 31 to 60 or higher. Check the keywords you already rank for to find your own reach.\nHow is keyword difficulty calculated? | Each tool studies the pages that rank in the top 10 and scores how strong they are, often using backlinks, domain and page authority, content quality and search results features. The formulas are private and differ by tool.\nIs keyword difficulty accurate? | It is a useful estimate, not a promise. Tools disagree, many lean heavily on links and none know your own site. Use it to make a shortlist and then read the real results.\nIs a low keyword difficulty always better? | No. Some easy keywords bring visitors who will never buy. Pick keywords that are winnable and relevant to your business, and check the search intent.\nWhat is the difference between keyword difficulty and search volume? | Search volume is how many times a month people search for a term. Keyword difficulty is how hard it is to rank for it. You need both to choose a keyword.\nCan I check keyword difficulty for free? | Yes. Free checkers show a score and volume for a few searches a day. You can also read the top 10 results yourself and use Google Search Console to find phrases you almost rank for.\nHow do I rank for a high-difficulty keyword? | Build a group of related pages, make the main page clearly better than those that rank, earn links from relevant sites, and expect it to take months. Start with easier related keywords to build trust.\nWhat is long-tail keyword difficulty? | Long-tail keywords are longer, more specific phrases. They usually have lower difficulty and clearer intent, which makes them a good starting point for newer sites.\nHow often should I recheck keyword difficulty? | Every three to six months, and before you update an older page. Results and scores change as new pages are published and links are earned.", "rl_sources": "Google Search Central: Creating helpful, reliable, people-first content | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content\nGoogle Search Central: Spam policies for Google web search | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies\nAhrefs: Keyword difficulty (reviewed for topics covered) | https://ahrefs.com/blog/keyword-difficulty/\nWritesonic: What is keyword difficulty (reviewed for topics covered) | https://writesonic.com/blog/what-is-keyword-difficulty\nWsCube Tech: Keyword difficulty (reviewed for topics covered) | https://wscubetech.com/blog/keyword-difficulty", "rl_g_glossary": "Keyword difficulty (KD) | A score, usually 0 to 100, that estimates how hard it is to rank on page one for a keyword.\nSearch intent | The reason behind a search: to learn, to compare or to buy.\nLong-tail keyword | A longer, narrower search phrase with lower volume and usually lower difficulty.\nBacklink | A link from another website to your page.\nTopic cluster | A group of related pages that link to one another to show depth on a subject.", "rl_g_cta_service": "services/best-search-engine-optimization-services", "rl_g_cta_head": "Want help choosing keywords you can win?", "rl_g_cta_sub": "Reinforce Lab starts with a free diagnostic of your Google and AI search visibility and a 90-day plan.", "rl_g_cta_button": "See SEO services", "_yoast_wpseo_focuskw": "keyword difficulty"}}